<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Simurrum</h1><p class="st-meta">2300 BCE – 1750 BCE (approx.) · capital Simurrum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bronze Age kingdom in the Zagros foothills east of the Tigris, repeatedly attacked by the kings of Akkad and Ur and known from its rulers' rock reliefs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Simurrum lay on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the Iranian highlands, probably near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-zab">Lesser Zab</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Sirwan</a> (upper Diyala); its capital has not been securely located. It appears in year names and inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, whose kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> campaigned against it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its internal life. It was a Hurrian-influenced hill kingdom with fortified towns controlling trade in metals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and horses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> boasted of destroying Simurrum several times. A local king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iddin-sin">Iddin-Sin</a>, and his son Zabazuna left Akkadian inscriptions and a rock relief celebrating their victories, revealing a brief period of independent power around 2000 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Simurrum disappears from the record in the 18th century BCE, when the region fell under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/simurrum.jpg" alt="Simurrum" width="320" height="255"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elam1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2300 BCE – 1750 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Simurrum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Iddin-Sin, Zabazuna</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Hurrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Simurum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2025&amp;focus=simurrum" aria-label="Simurrum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5765 977 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Simurrum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0M6193 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6085 1233h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6085 1233h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-2025&amp;focus=simurrum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

